From 3143a2ebae570e982199599adfd986ff8366ba0e Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Artyom Shalkhakov <artyom.shalkhakov@gmail.com>
Date: Mon, 19 Oct 2026 16:49:44 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] Answer selection markers from a controller's selection

NSObjectController's -selection answered its content, and
NSArrayController's the selected object when exactly one was selected and
nil otherwise. So selection.<key> was nil with nothing selected and with
several objects selected, and a control bound through it showed an empty
value, enabled, where AppKit shows its "No Selection" or "Multiple Values"
placeholder; the binding code here already handles the markers, but never
received one.

On macOS -selection answers a proxy whose -valueForKey: is the selected
objects' value when they agree on it, NSMultipleValuesMarker when they do
not (or always for several, with alwaysUsesMultipleValuesMarker), and
NSNoSelectionMarker when nothing is selected; setting a value through it
sets it on every selected object; and observers of selection.<key> are
told when that value changes, whether the selection changes or a selected
object's value does (checked on macOS 15). -selection now answers such a
proxy, one per controller, for both classes. It registers observations
made through it on the objects selected at the time; the key-value
observing in gnustep-base hands an object that implements
-addObserver:forKeyPath:options:context: the rest of a key path, and moves
the observation to the new selection whenever selection changes.

A key the selected objects do not have answers NSNotApplicableMarker,
which the binding code turns into its placeholder, or an exception when
the binding asks for NSRaisesForNotApplicableKeys. On macOS the proxy
itself raises and the binding substitutes the marker; here the binding
reads its new value from the change dictionary, which key-value observing
fills by asking the proxy, so the proxy answers the marker.

A marker that reaches a binding is replaced by a placeholder: the
binding's option for that marker, or else the default, which was "No
Selection", "Multiple Values" or "Not Applicable" for every binding. On
macOS the default is +defaultPlaceholderForMarker:withBinding: of the
bound object's class, nil for every class and binding but NSTextField's
value, which has those three strings (checked on macOS 15). With a
string for its default, content bound through a selection became the
string: an array controller whose contentSet is bound to another's
selection.records failed to load from its nib, "NSConstantString
(instance) does not recognize sortedArrayUsingDescriptors:", and an
NSTableView's or an NSPopUpButton's content, a table column's font and a
text view's value would have been given the string too. On macOS they
get nil, so content through no selection or multiple values is empty.
+defaultPlaceholderForMarker:withBinding: and
+setDefaultPlaceholder:forMarker:withBinding:, declared here but never
implemented, now keep a placeholder per class, binding and marker,
inherited by subclasses; a binding without the option uses its bound
object's, and NSTextField sets its value's three.

Tests/gui/NSObjectController/selectionMarkers.m covers each marker, values
set through the selection, and the notifications; eleven of its seventeen
checks fail without the change. Tests/gui/NSArrayController/selectionKVO.m
checked that -selection was the selected object itself and nil for no
selection; it now checks what AppKit gives instead.
Tests/gui/NSObjectController/markerPlaceholders.m binds an array
controller's contentSet and contentArray, an object controller's
contentObject, a popup's and a table's content and a text field's value
through a selection, with and without placeholder options, and checks
the default placeholders; it passes on macOS 15. Without the change six
of its checks fail, and its second set stops at the first call to
+defaultPlaceholderForMarker:withBinding:, not implemented.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
